Yes—**I2P+ with the ready-to-use extras**, not just a browser profile pointed at a proxy. I understand the goal as making I2P a built-in mode beside regular browsing, with its router integration and useful I2P destinations available from the start.

One concrete example: I2P+ describes a curated set of Router Console links and an address-book subscription for resolving those `.i2p` names. Synapse could make that setup automatic, then show the sites only when the I2P router is ready—while clearly showing connection status and never falling back to direct browsing if the route fails. [I2P+](https://i2pplus.github.io/) · [I2P+ hosts list](https://i2pplus.github.io/hosts.txt)

Bundling or distributing the I2P+ router itself would also need a licensing and update review; its source repository identifies it as AGPL-3.0. [I2P+ source](https://github.com/I2PPlus/i2pplus/)

I was partway through wiring a useful I2P first-run page and an optional I2P+ address-book subscription shortcut. I’ll first reconcile the edits already on disk—one launch-handler change may need correction—then run the focused contract/build check and update the README.
