{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p2-968e1fff","text":"**Considering integration methods**\n\nThe user seems to want to know how to incorporate a feature into settings pages, possibly asking for a concrete suggestion or design architecture. I might look into Firefox’s about:preferences settings files for insight. It sounds like they’re looking for guidance on integrating options pages and user experience, particularly around managing installs or routers. I’ll need to review current Mozilla settings in upstream and the workspace, but I can only work within the provided project path.**Exploring integration options**\n\nI’m reflecting on the user's interest in integrating I2P+. It seems I might need to look at repo policies or the architecture for extensions. I’ve already conducted searches and found preferences policies, but maybe I can find existing settings UI files in the overlay. It feels a bit straightforward, but I want to engage properly. Perhaps I could propose a section in Settings > Privacy & Security for I2P+, managing installations, and using a technical backend service manager, distinguishing between browser UI settings and the router console.**Clarifying settings and controls**\n\nI need to ensure that settings controls are streamlined, allowing only advanced proxy configurations to prevent failures. I’ll focus on the installation flow, bundled choices, and service states. The network proxy will be set to 127.0.0.1:4444, with the router control port configured. For macOS, I shouldn’t require system privileges; instead, it might run per-user. I’m considering a Windows service but noting potential admin overhead. I also want to keep the host feed and subscription options clear, ensuring an explicit opt-in.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"}
Yes. I’d add I2P+ to **Settings → Privacy & Security → I2P Network**, so people can install and manage it without leaving Synapse. The current macOS integration is command-line only; [the launcher](tools/synapse-macos.sh) expects a router that’s already running, so settings integration would be a new step.

**Start with a settings-managed MVP** that can detect an existing I2P+ router, start and stop it, show whether it’s ready, and open the I2P browser or router console. Keep the I2P browsing profile separate from normal profiles, and make “start the router automatically” an explicit, off-by-default option. A TCP listener alone shouldn’t be presented as “ready”; the status should distinguish “running” from “bootstrapped.”

Then add **Install I2P+** to that same page. The installer can offer the bundled router and its Java runtime, explain the licenses, and manage updates and removal. Keep router data separate from browser profiles so that removing the browser or router can have clear, deliberate effects on user data.

Technically, the settings page should ask a **privileged Synapse component** to install, start, stop, or query the router; it shouldn’t execute shell commands itself. Bind the router and proxy to loopback, and keep I2P browsing unavailable unless the required proxy is ready. Keep the optional I2P+ address-book subscription opt-in, as documented in the [privacy notes](docs/PRIVACY.md).

I2P+’s AGPL license permits redistribution subject to its conditions, but bundling also means handling source and notices for the exact version shipped, plus checking the Java runtime’s separate license. I’d make the installer component distinct and review the packaging/licensing design before release.

The natural next step is to build the settings panel around the MVP controls, while leaving bundled installation for a follow-up.

