{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p15-55beab37","text":"Now I can see the screenshot. The title bar strip (top, with traffic lights and the "New Tab" tab) renders in a **muted mauve/gray-purple** (system vibrancy), while the toolbox below (nav bar, tab strip behind) is our dark indigo. The mac titlebar uses `-moz-default-appearance`? Actually this is the classic macOS issue: the *titlebar* portion (tabs row) comes from the window server with a translucent gray because... Interesting: toolbox body background: `var(--toolbox-background-color)` = our indigo. But the visible top strip is lighter. Why?

Explanation: On macOS, when `-moz-mac-tahoe-theme` and customtitlebar, `body::after` matched with `-moz-default-appearance: -moz-window-titlebar` — gated on `-moz-native-theme` which should be false (pref default false)... but wait: does anything set `browser.theme.native-theme = true`? Check StaticPrefList: value false for non-GTK. Yet the user's screenshot shows a native-looking gray titlebar (that mauve = system gray tint of macOS Tahoe light mode).

So maybe the media query isn't the mechanism for the titlebar strip. Actually the strip with traffic lights IS the tab strip (tab bar row). Its background... `#TabsToolbar` `.browser-toolbar:not(.browser-titlebar) background-color: var(--toolbar-background-color)`... hmm.

Wait — in the screenshot, the tab strip row appears mauve-gray (light) and the row *below* (with back/forward + url bar) is dark indigo, and the whole bottom content area is *very* dark (nearly black). Interesting: content ~#0a0a12-ish. Everything looks right except the title strip and ALSO the "active tab" is highlighted white-ish. Now the tab strip's lighter shade suggests the tabstrip toolbar keeps its own appearance: `#TabsToolbar` has class `browser-titlebar` → background transparent? Then body bg shows through — but body bg = indigo... unless the body pseudo-element uses native appearance after all.

Hold on: maybe `browser.theme.native-theme` pref was flipped: nsXPLookAndFeel lists it under `kLookAndFeelIntPrefs`... default false (non-GTK). Hmm.

Alternatively the mauve is `-moz-default-appearance: -moz-window-titlebar` from `body::after` because `@media (-moz-platform: macos) and (-moz-native-theme)` — if this is matching, native theme is true. Note: `-moz-native-theme` might evaluate *true* per `forceNonNativeTheme` false and pref — hmm, but macOS default false. But wait: maybe the check `eval_moz_native_theme` also returns pref OR `document.mForceNonNativeTheme`. It returns `crate::pref!("browser.theme.native-theme")` — the static default false. So false. Then how is the strip gray?

Look again at the screenshot: gradient from left (grayish) across — actually the top strip fades from gray to purple toward the right? It looks like a translucent light gray over dark = mauve. This could be macOS Tahoe *window background vibrancy* showing through: Firefox on macOS uses `-moz-window-titlebar` "appearance auto" only in that native-theme branch... but ALSO the tab bar drag area may have vibrancy/background-alpha from the system when the toolbox background is transparent.

Alternatively, our `--toolbox-background-color` IS applying (the strip shows *blend*)?? The mauve = light gray system bg blended 50% with indigo? That's exactly what a translucent window background gives — macOS Tahoe default light window background is a gray; our chrome is drawn with alpha? `--toolbox-background-color: #100b28` is opaque... but if body::after uses native appearance *in the titlebar area* the strip is opaque gray.

OK rather than keep reverse-engineering pixel-blends, maybe simplest robust fix: give the browser window an explicit `appearance: none` on body and titlebar and set `background: var(--toolbox-background-color)` with `!important`... but if the strip comes from native appearance via `-moz-default-appearance`, overriding in our theme stylesheet with:

```
@media (-moz-platform: macos) {
  :root[customtitlebar]:not([lwtheme]) body::after { appearance: none !important; }
}
```
Hmm but browser-shared.css rule is `@media (-moz-platform: macos) and (-moz-native-theme)` — if that branch isn't active, something else draws the strip.

WAIT. I bet the strip IS the body background, but... the user's system is Light mode. `light-dark()`! `--toolbox-background-color` — we set it directly (opaque #100b28) under `:root:not([lwtheme])` guarded by `not ((forced-colors) or (-moz-native-theme))`. If that media is NOT matching (e.g., native-theme true), our whole theme doesn't apply: then toolbox bg falls back to browser-colors dark defaults: `--toolbox-background-color: light-dark(rgb(237,...), rgb(28,27,34))` — light-dired evaluated per prefers-color-scheme, which in Light mode = light gray #ebedef-ish. That would make the toolbox light gray — but the screenshot's *below* rows are dark...

Hmm, in the screenshot: ONLY the top strip is light; the nav row and toolbox below are dark. So the top strip uses a different background source than the rest.

Actually looking once more at the traffic-light strip: it might be **the macOS window titlebar area OUTSIDE the web content**, i.e., customtitlebar==false → native system titlebar strip (light gray in Tahoe light) sits above, and the indigo tab strip is beneath it. But the screenshot's strip contains the *tab* ("New Tab" with close X and the + button), meaning the tab strip IS in the titlebar row — mauve. And tab selected = white with focus ring (native-ish!). The + button is white. The whole strip looks system-nativish.

Also the tab has a cyan outline — our focus ring? The tab outline `--focus-outline-color: #4de7f5`... our cyan! In the screenshot the tab's border is cyan-ish. So our vars partially apply (focus outline) — meaning theme IS loaded. So the strip: mauve = ? 

Hmm! What about `--toolbox-background-color` on `body` = #100b28 (very dark). The strip shows mauve → the strip's bg isn't body. #TabsToolbar might have its own style: `.browser-toolbar:not(.browser-titlebar) { background: var(--toolbar-background-color) }` — so titlebar-area toolbar is transparent → body bg shows = dark. That contradicts.

Unless the customtitlebar branch (`-moz-native-theme` matching) DOES apply: body bg transparent in titlebar area… no wait, that rule makes `body` background transparent only when customtitlebar, then `::after` draws native titlebar — the whole *window background* becomes the native titlebar appearance — the entire window would be gray, not just the strip.

Alright, empirical debugging beats archaeology. Firefox has remote debugging; the devtools chrome inspector could tell us computed styles. That's heavy. Alternative pragmatic fix that usually works for this exact macOS-Tahoe symptom: `default-preferences` set `browser.theme.native-theme` — no...

Actually — I remember now! This exact mauve/purple-gray strip on macOS Tahoe occurs when the **window is inactive**? No...

Another real known cause: macOS Tahoe "Liquid Glass" beta builds render Firefox titlebar with native vibrancy when `browser.tabs.inTitlebar` true and *window buttons row has its own strip* on Tahoe: Tahoe windows have a "title bar accessory" area. On Tahoe (macOS 26), Firefox added `browser.tabs.inTitlebar` handling with native `NSTitlebarAccessoryViewController`? The mauve tint = Tahoe's systemUnderPageBackground blended with our toolbox color under it. The strip is drawn by AppKit with the "titlebar" material (translucent light gray), and our toolbox color shows *through* it → indigo mixed with gray = mauve! That matches the pixel look: translucent material over dark = mauve-gray.

Fix: make that native material not used — that's precisely what the `-moz-default-appearance: -moz-window-titlebar` / `appearance: auto` does; while `appearance: none` + explicit bg gives flat color. Since it currently looks translucent (material over dark), the native appearance IS active. If `-moz-native-theme` didn't match, appearance auto wouldn't be set... unless a macOS Tahoe-specific build (158 with Tahoe theme) draws native always with `browser.theme.native-theme` defaulting differently in this branch (their source may have changed default to true on macOS Tahoe!). This is a custom Firefox 158.0a1 from 2026 — plausibly Tahoe defaults native theme ON for macOS.

Robust fix: force non-native theme chrome ourselves: set pref `browser.theme.native-theme` = false in synapse.js; that flips `-moz-native-theme` to false → the native titlebar appearance never engages, our `body` background stays opaque indigo. That single pref likely fixes both the strip and the light-dark() evaluation mismatches (e.g., tab selected white-ish which came from light-dark light side!).

Note also `--tab-background-color-selected: light-dark(var(--color-white), rgba(...))` from tokens-browser-theme — in Light appearance → white tab header, which EXACTLY matches the screenshot's white active tab while our overridden `--tab-background-color-selected: #211d45` apparently didn't hit (because our media `(not -moz-native-theme)` didn't match!). Everything consistent: `-moz-native-theme` MATCHES on this build → our entire theme vars never applied in the *browser-colors* layer for tokens using light-dark... but focus outline cyan applied? Hmm, cyan outline could be Firefox's default in-app lwtheme? Eh.

Wait, if native-theme matched, our whole synapse-theme.css would be inert (single outer media). Then where does cyan border come from? Nova? `--tab-border-color-accent: linear-gradient(...violet/orange)` not cyan. Hmm, the cyan line might be the *focused tab* ring `--focus-outline-color` in dark → cyan? Firefox dark default `--focus-outline-color` is `#00ddff` (cyan) per browser-colors lwtheme block! And light-dark evaluation: `light-dark(#0061e0, #00ddff)` picks by *preferred color scheme of the document* — chrome documents on macOS follow... the *widget theme* (which is native). Chrome doc prefers-color-scheme = light in Light mode?? Then #0061e0 blue, not cyan.

I can't fully resolve by static analysis. Faster: probe the live browser. It's running (pid 10355 dev instance + maybe user's 10132). Chrome devtools via remote debugging port: relaunch with `--remote-debugging-port` then query computed styles via CDP evaluate on the chrome document? CDP covers web content; chrome window pages are also CDP targets though (browser chrome not). Too deep.

 quickest decisive probe: `mach run` again? No.

Direct approach: check what pref value the *running build* has for browser.theme.native-theme and whether this branch changed the default: grep StaticPrefs for MOZ_WIDGET... already did: false for non-GTK. But ALSO check `pref mozilla-central branch` — it's the same file I read. OK so native theme pref is false.

Hmm... so `-moz-native-theme` false... then `body::after` native appearance NOT active. The strip mauve would then be... Let me reconsider: maybe the strip IS transparent-ish showing the *desktop wallpaper* through a vibrancy effect? The strip fades from lighter left to purple right — wallpaper-dependent! macOS Tahoe liquid-glass titlebar vibrancy over the gecko background. That means the native strip is drawn by AppKit as a *layered material* regardless of CSS (Tahoe). Firefox 158 on Tahoe might enable this material when `browser.theme.native-theme` false? There were Firefox Tahoecompatibility notes about `NSWindow` titlebar in bugzilla.

Given the uncertainty, the highest-leverage deterministic move: set `--toolbox-background-color` etc. AND explicitly disable native appearance for the titlebar pseudo-element and tabs area in our theme CSS with `appearance: none !important` on `body::after` under macOS customtitlebar, plus give `#TabsToolbar` and `#nav-bar` explicit opaque backgrounds. CSS override in our stylesheet loaded AFTER browser-shared.css → same specificity ties? `!important` wins regardless.

Also set pref `browser.theme.native-theme` false explicitly in synapse.js for determinism.

And per the user's answer: they ALSO want the palette re-tuned to krypton-lang.org: purple #7722ff family rather than midnight/cyan. From the screenshot, dominant chrome is dark; keep dark but purple-accent:

- toolbox bg: #0a0a0f (kryptonbytes bg) or #0f0f17? Use #0a0a0f for the title bar area (nav-bg), toolbar row #0f0f17, fields #0b0b15, cards #1a1a26.
- accent: #7722ff primary; attention/hover #8842ff; links #a974ff (violet-light) hover #c81cff magenta? links maybe #a974ff.
- text #f5f5fb, secondary #9a9ab2.
- tab selected: #1a1a26 with border top accent #7722ff.
- focus ring: #7722ff.
- green #3ddc84 for attention? kryptonbytes --green:#3ddc84.
- separators: rgba(255,255,255,0.09).

Let me rewrite synapse-theme.css accordingly, set pref in synapse.js, rebuild, relaunch, then ask user to look.

Also the user's earlier mystery: title bar strip. Our new CSS will override with `appearance: none !important` for the macOS titlebar pseudo element. Add:

```
@media (-moz-platform: macos) {
  :root:not([lwtheme]) body::after { appearance: none !important; background-color: var(--toolbox-background-color) !important; }
}
```
Hmm — careful: that rule in browser-shared only exists inside native-theme media; outside, body::after has content:"" positioned but appearance default none anyway... Setting background on ::after could double-paint; body bg already set. The strip's native pixel origin being AppKit can't be beaten by CSS if it's really a system material. But if it's CSS native appearance, `appearance:none` wins.

Plan of record for CSS changes in synapse-theme.css:
1. Keep `@media not ((forced-colors) or (-moz-native-theme))` guard.
2. Add macOS-specific block with `!important` appearance:none for body::after + explicit backgrounds for titlebar rows.
3. Update palette to Krypton purple.

Also light mode: user's macOS is Light; tokens use `light-dark()` → light variants would take over tokens NOT overridden. We override the key chrome tokens with fixed dark values — good; chrome stays dark regardless of system light appearance. That matches "dark browser chrome" expectation and fixes the white selected tab.

Write the new stylesheet now.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"}
Now I can see it: the title-bar strip renders as a light system-tinted material (muted mauve) while the rest of the chrome is dark, and the selected tab goes white because Gecko's `light-dark()` tokens still follow the system Light appearance in the top strip. I'll fix both: force the titlebar off native appearance, and re-tune the whole palette to your Krypton scheme (`#7722ff`/`#a974ff` purple on near-black `#0a0a0f`/`#0f0f17` surfaces) from krypton-lang.org and kryptonbytes.com.
{"kind":"tool","id":"iJbfOwOfPbQ","toolName":"write_file","input":{"path":"overlay/browser/themes/shared/synapse-theme.css","instructions":"Re-tune the Synapse chrome palette to the Krypton brand scheme and force the macOS titlebar off native appearance","content":"/* SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 */

/* Synapse browser chrome palette, tuned to the Krypton brand scheme
 * (krypton-lang.org, kryptonbytes.com): Krypton violet on near-black
 * indigo surfaces. Fixed dark chrome values so the top bar matches the
 * toolbars regardless of the system light/dark appearance, while
 * Firefox's forced-colors and high-contrast modes stay authoritative. */
@media not ((forced-colors) or (-moz-native-theme)) {
  :root:not([lwtheme]) {
    /* Surfaces: kryptonbytes.com --nav-bg, --bg, --code-bg, cards. */
    --toolbox-background-color: #0a0a0f;
