Found and fixed the root cause. Here's why the pages didn't match:

**Why it happened:** In this Firefox version, the new-tab page isn't driven by the `browser.newtab.url` pref alone. The override only takes effect when the browser *actively applies* it at runtime (which normally happens only from the Settings pane or an extension). A distribution default pref was silently ignored — so every new tab fell back to the built-in Firefox Home while Safari went straight to `krypton-lang.org/s.html`. Once you typed the address directly, both browsers loaded the same real start page, which is why they matched on a direct navigation.

**What I changed:** I patched the front-end (`AboutNewTab.sys.mjs`) so the browser now honors `browser.newtab.url` on startup and live as it changes — meaning:

- New tabs now open `https://www.krypton-lang.org/s.html` by default, just like startup.
- Users can still override it — via Settings' smaller controls, an extension computing its own new tab, or by editing `browser.newtab.url` in `about:config` (it applies immediately without a restart).
- If the URL is set back to `about:newtab`, the built-in Firefox Home returns gracefully.

It's tracked as a reusable patch in the overlay pipeline at [synapse-newtaburl.patch](overlay/privacy/synapse-newtaburl.patch) and wired into the build at [synapse-macos.sh](tools/synapse-macos.sh), so future overlays/builds reproduce it, not just this one-off tree edit.

**What's live:** Synapse is rebuilt, freshly packaged, reinstalled under `/Applications`, relaunched, and running (PID 40828). Open a new tab — it should land on the exact start page Safari shows, with the same search bar, engine selection ("DuckDuckGo ⇄"), and Favorites row.⛕The user wants me to suggest follow-up prompts. Keep it concise and end the turn now.That's the full pass: new tabs now open the Krypton start page like Safari does, with the same search bar and engine picker. Open a new tab to confirm — and if anything's still off, grab a screenshot.
