All green, and the root cause of *both* symptoms turned out to be the same bug:

**What was happening on your machine:** the "Start Page" label module wraps the address bar's `setURI` method. That wrapper was calling the original method the wrong way — as a plain call, which loses the object context (`this`) inside it. So every time any code touched the address bar state, deep inside Gecko's URL bar, `this.#isAddressbar` blew up with `TypeError: can't convert undefined to object`. That crash aborted the first tab's whole startup sequence, so the first tab never got its label *or* its page — until you opened a second tab, whose tab-switch path re-ran the logic and worked.

**Fixed along the way:**

- The setURI wrapper now preserves the receiver — the TypeError at UrlbarInputBase.mjs:975 is gone entirely, and it was never Gecko's bug.
- The first-tab startup sweep now schedules its next tick *before* doing work, so even a transient error (or a slow network, which we saw earlier that day) can't kill the loop; and it now stops itself shortly after the first tab settles instead of running for a full minute.
- `updateLabel` is wrapped so no transient issue can break a navigation/progress callback chain.

**Verified with two fresh-profile launches against the installed app:** zero JavaScript errors in the log, first tab at index 0 loads `krypton-lang.org/s.html`, doc commits, and `overridden=true value="Start Page"` asserts right on that first tab, no second tab needed. The running window on your side is the test profile instance — close it and just launch Synapse normally; the installed app picks this up live (the chrome file is symlinked, no rebuild needed).
